WEBVTT

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:42.680
There's an insect in this garden that all gardeners loathe.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:43.680
Aphids.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:51.400
They've made enemies of gardeners, but in the undergrowth they have friends.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.400
Ants.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:01:04.120
Ants herd aphids to the best possible feeding places, just as human shepherds will herd

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.880
their sheep to the best pastures.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.440
And just as shepherds protect their flocks against wolves, so ants protect the aphids

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.320
against their insect enemies.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.960
Ladybirds are among the most dangerous.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.320
They after all eat aphids.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.760
So the ants must get rid of them.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:24.800
That's not easy.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:33.600
It's quite hard to get a grip on the polished shell of a ladybird.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.160
But eventually, success.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.680
Aphids excrete a liquid that ants relish, honeydew.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.680
That's why ants protect them.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.160
Such close relationships are frequent among insects, perhaps because they've had so long

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.760
to develop them.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.760
They appeared on land after all about 100 million years before any backboneed animal.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.600
And they can also evolve much faster because they can produce several generations within

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.240
a single year.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.840
So perhaps it's not surprising that they have developed relationships between one another

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:21.680
of a complexity that blows the mind.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.320
These associations extend not only to other insects, but to plants.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.000
They were established at a very early period.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.640
Plants are the basis of all life, for only they can combine minerals in the ground with

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.080
gases from the air and produce something worth eating.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:54.400
Insects however, not only eat them, they also exploit them in much more devious ways.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.840
Tropical rainforests are famous for being thick, tangled masses of vegetation.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:08.960
But in this one in Peru, there are mysterious clearings where only one or at most two kinds

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.280
of trees will grow.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:18.080
The local people call such places as this devil's gardens and believe that spirits

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.920
kill other kinds of trees.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:27.720
And the real killers of those trees, well, they've only just been discovered.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:34.680
The leaves of the surviving trees all have these swellings on their stems.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:41.720
And going in and out are armies of tiny, tiny ants.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.880
The swellings are their homes, specially developed for them by the tree.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:51.000
And in them, safe from predators, the ants keep their eggs and larvae.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:57.320
They even keep domestic livestock, white scale insects, which like aphids, supply the ants

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:02.560
with drinks of honeydew.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.360
Producing this accommodation also benefits the tree, for the ants provide their landlord

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:08.920
with a valuable service.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:15.120
They guard it against its enemies.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:20.600
All kinds of insects will eat a plant's leaves, given the chance.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:27.440
But they don't get a chance, not on this tree.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:33.480
So the caterpillar goes elsewhere.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:38.920
This is a more formidable leaf muncher, a kind of giant grasshopper, several thousand

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:44.280
times bigger than any individual ant.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:53.440
It's not so easy to shift, but it does have a weak spot.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:05:05.160
If you can say that any insect has a heel, then this one has an Achilles heel.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:15.520
The ants seem to know it.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.760
Enough is enough.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:26.360
The ants not only repel their hosts' animal enemies, they also, perhaps more remarkably,

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:30.440
keep competing plants at bay.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:38.920
A squad of them leaves the barracks and sets out on one of their regular patrols of the

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:41.360
neighborhood.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.720
They found a newly sprouted sapling.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:50.320
Perhaps it's grown from one of their landlord's seeds, in which case all well and good.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:51.840
But this one hasn't.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.520
It's an intruder.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:06:02.200
They go into action, biting its stems.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.200
Reinforcements arrive.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:09.280
Hundreds of tiny jaws cut into its stems.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:25.300
Everything begins to wilt.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:29.560
But bites alone are not enough for the ants to achieve their ends.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:40.720
They lift their abdomens and inject formic acid into the crippled plant's wounds.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:49.800
The poison spreads through the plant's tissues, hastening its death.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:57.200
And within a few days of being comprehensively stung, all these plants are dead.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:04.800
And the ants, or the devils, have extended their garden still further.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.040
But the benefit of this drastic gardening, of course, is not restricted to the plants.

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:12.640
The ants also profit.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:18.240
They have ensured that their plant landlord can extend its territory without competition.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:33.400
And that provides them with more homes, so they too can increase their numbers.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:39.460
It's one thing to provide food and shelter in return for protection, but it's quite another

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:44.360
thing to be compelled to provide a home where before there was none.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:49.640
But some insects have the ability to force a plant to do just that.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:55.480
They're called gallmakers, and this oak tree is infested with them.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:08:02.400
This odd wrinkled object at the base of an acorn is known as a nopper gall.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.360
Inside there's the tiny grub of a minute wasp.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:18.000
To understand how it got there, we have to go back to last spring.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:24.480
This tiny insect, scarcely bigger than a mosquito, is one of these gall wasps.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:31.720
There are lots of them flying around the oak flowers.

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:37.000
Most of the flowers by now have been pollinated and are about to develop into acorns.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:44.960
The gall wasps too have mated, and this female is looking for a place to lay her eggs.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:52.480
She thrusts her ovipositor into the base of the fertilized flower and injects an egg.

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:59.040
And that triggers a profound genetic change in the growing oak bud.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:07.280
It develops not into an acorn, but into something very different, a gall.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:13.640
Within the tiny larva whose secretions have caused the change feeds on the oak tree's

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:14.640
tissues.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:27.440
As summer proceeds, the galls become increasingly hard and woody.

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:31.480
Autumn comes and the oak tree starts to shed its leaves.

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:33.520
It's shutting down for the winter.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:55.880
And with its leaves go both acorns and galls.

00:09:55.880 --> 00:10:01.520
Spring and insect life is suspended.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:08.440
But unseen changes are nevertheless taking place.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:18.440
Spring comes at last.

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:22.000
Inside the gall, something starts moving.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:32.440
The larva has turned into an adult wasp.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:36.160
It has spent nine months within the oak tree's tissues.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:42.240
It has only a few weeks of its life left.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:55.240
Now, as an adult, it must look for another oak to inject with eggs.

00:10:55.240 --> 00:11:04.600
A single oak tree may be afflicted by 70 different kinds of gall, each produced by different

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:14.120
species of wasp, and each with its own particular contorted shape.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:19.200
These hard shells may seem to be effective defenses for the little grub inside them,

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:24.000
but not necessarily so.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:30.160
This is another kind of gall wasp, and she's not a genetic engineer, she's a burglar.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:35.240
Behind her, she trails her equipment for breaking and entering a drill.

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:43.320
She carefully selects a site for her operations and takes aim.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:47.480
She flicks away the drill's sheath and starts work.

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:54.280
Her aim has to be very accurate if she's to strike her target, the larva at the gall

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:55.280
center.

00:11:55.280 --> 00:12:01.880
The tip of her drill has a sharp cutting edge of metallic zinc, which pierces the gall tissues

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:04.320
with ease.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:09.320
When she detects that she's reached the central chamber, a microscopic egg travels down the

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:15.360
center of the drill and into the larva.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:17.480
The operation is over.

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:22.880
Her offspring will now hatch in the gall center, consume the flesh of the resident larva, and

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:30.480
take over the gall.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:31.480
Galls are worldwide.

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:39.720
California, for example, has other species of oak tree and other kinds of gall.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:44.080
These particular ones are relatively tiny, the size of peppercorns.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:48.040
You'll hardly notice them, except for one thing.

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:55.720
They jump, and not only do they jump, they jump for three days.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:13:00.920
The tiny larvae within flip themselves about inside their minute chambers.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:02.720
Why they should do so is not clear.

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:07.920
Perhaps it's a way of moving their homes into cracks and crevices where they're out of the

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:14.540
reach of predators and parasites and shaded from the hot Californian sun.

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:19.120
The gall in Hungary protects itself in a more complex fashion.

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:23.120
It recruits insect guardians.

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:27.360
This gall is producing nectar.

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:31.120
It's sweet.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:36.360
And it's producing it not for the benefit of the oak tree, but for the benefit of the

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:40.320
tiny grub that lies within the gall.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:48.360
Because the nectar attracts ants, and ants serve as defenders against any other intruders.

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:57.800
And if you want to see how valuable they are, let me remove some.

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:02.280
Within a few minutes, a different kind of gall wasp appears.

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:07.080
It's another of those burglars looking for an existing gall into which it can inject

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:09.420
its egg.

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:18.840
But the ants have now returned, and they attack the intruder.

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:21.480
Away it goes.

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:30.200
The ants, having driven off the wasp, take their reward of nectar.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:35.240
In the normal course of events, oak trees don't produce nectar, but many plants certainly

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:36.320
do.

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:41.600
It's a way of attracting insects that will transport their pollen from one plant to another.

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:52.240
And the colorful flowers are advertisements proclaiming that nectar is there for the taking.

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:57.920
But the plants must also ensure that visiting insects collect the pollen as well as nectar,

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:06.320
and that leads to all kinds of complexities.

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:11.960
Like many plants, the pyramidal orchid has a way of ensuring that they do.

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:18.200
A burnet moth probes into the orchid's nectar store, and as it does so, a horseshoe-shaped

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:24.320
mass of pollen clips onto its long proboscis.

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:32.360
Inconvenient it may be, but the moth can't shift it.

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:37.000
Away it goes to another flower, taking the pollen with it.

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:43.720
And this time, as it probes for the drink, a speck of pollen is transferred to the female

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:46.720
part of the flower.

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:51.200
The job is done.

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:57.040
The traffic of insect pollinators to and from flowers is so heavy, and in particular so

00:15:57.040 --> 00:16:03.000
predictable, that it's not surprising that some invertebrates have learned to exploit

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:04.000
it.

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:12.080
A white crab spider sits almost invisible on a white flower, waiting in ambush.

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:18.780
And it catches a bee.

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:24.000
The spider is clearly taking advantage of the flower's advertising.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:28.800
It looks superbly camouflaged through our eyes, but insect eyes are different to ours

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:33.320
and see parts of the light spectrum invisible to us.

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:39.160
Under ultraviolet light, we can get a better idea of how they see things, and most surprisingly,

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:43.320
the spider looks more obvious to them than it does to us.

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:45.320
Why should that be?

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:51.080
Perhaps it's because ultraviolet markings on some flowers serve to guide insects to

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:52.080
nectar.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:58.560
So maybe the spider's color is a positive attraction for bees.

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:04.600
Certainly, honey bees seem more likely to visit flowers with crab spiders on them than

00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:15.040
those without, often with fatal consequences.

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:19.880
The relationships between the animals that live in the undergrowth are full of such deceits

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:20.880
and imposters.

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:28.200
Here in Australia, there's an intriguing example that has only just been discovered.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:36.480
This is a feather-legged bug.

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:43.720
It too manages to persuade prey to come close, but its invitations are aimed not at bees,

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:50.880
but at ants, and what the ants get is a very nasty surprise.

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:56.640
Like all members of the bug family, this one has a long tube for a mouth.

00:17:56.640 --> 00:18:00.200
Most stick it into plants to suck sap.

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:04.320
Using it to eat an ant is more difficult.

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:08.600
The bug starts by waving to passing ants.

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:19.280
The feathery flanges on its legs are so large they can be seen from quite a distance.

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:24.840
The ants are visibly intrigued, but they're not yet close enough for the bug to attack,

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:32.880
so it reinforces its gestures by producing a chemical perfume that the ants find irresistible.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:37.800
They come closer still.

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:43.360
They climb all over the bug, trying to find the source of this strange compulsive smell,

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:57.400
and the bug does nothing to stop them.

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:00.120
Where does that smell come from?

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:12.720
Is it on the bug's legs?

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:16.120
The bug now answers the ants' questions.

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:20.760
It lifts itself up and reveals a gland on its underside.

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:26.200
That's what's producing the smell.

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:31.400
The ant presses its head against the bug's chest to actually taste the gland.

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:37.160
It's the perfect position for its own execution.

00:19:37.160 --> 00:19:46.520
The bug stabs its mouth into the back of the ant's head.

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:53.560
So a tube can be used to suck nourishment from an insect as well as from a plant.

00:19:53.560 --> 00:20:02.640
This is the rogue of the bug family, a killer.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:09.720
Ants are among the most numerous, widespread and frequently exploited members of the undergrowth.

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:14.960
These in Australia collect seeds and store them underground.

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:20.240
Plants encourage them to do so by adding a tasty capsule to their seeds.

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:24.920
That may seem odd, but these ants don't eat all the seeds they store.

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:29.880
In fact, seeds are more likely to germinate below ground than above.

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:35.160
But not everything on this forest floor is what it seems.

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:42.600
When it comes to putting your eggs in a suitable place, some insects persuade other insects

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:45.240
to do the job for them.

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:52.280
This little object looks like a seed and certainly it's fallen from above and that ant seems

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:53.680
to think it's worth eating.

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:57.000
But actually it hasn't come from a plant.

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:01.960
It's come from another insect.

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:03.800
And this is it.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:09.760
It's rather difficult to see because it looks exactly like a dry leaf, but it's a stick

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:10.760
insect.

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:17.880
There's its head, antennae and that's the tip of its abdomen.

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:23.200
As an adult like this, it spends all its time up in the trees eating leaves.

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:29.560
And when the time comes to lay, and this one is doing so, all she does is simply to flick

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:32.560
away the egg and let it fall to the ground.

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:39.960
But that's not quite as risky as you might think.

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:45.960
Wherever you are, you can be pretty sure that some ants will turn up looking for food.

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:50.800
And that is exactly what the stick insect's eggs look like, a nutritious seed complete

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:55.480
with that fatty capsule at the tip.

00:21:55.480 --> 00:22:15.080
So the ants start to haul them away.

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:20.160
Although the ants certainly eat a great number of the seeds they store, stick insect eggs

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:22.280
don't seem to be quite as tempting.

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:32.960
At any rate, the ants, after all their labor, usually leave the stick insect eggs untouched.

00:22:32.960 --> 00:22:37.960
While the seasons pass, the eggs lie underground, hidden from birds and any other predators

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:39.440
that might eat them.

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:51.520
They may remain there safe for up to three years, but eventually they hatch.

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:59.520
It's only at this early stage of its life that a stick insect actually runs.

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:09.640
The youngsters positively scamper up into the tree branches.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:14.720
There they will take up their adult life of leisure, well camouflaged, stolidly chewing

00:23:14.720 --> 00:23:17.920
leaves.

00:23:17.920 --> 00:23:22.680
Giving your offspring a good start in life can take a lot of effort, so some insects

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:28.400
have evolved highly complex strategies to induce other species to become nursemaids

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:32.300
on their behalf.

00:23:32.300 --> 00:23:38.620
This Californian desert hardly seems to be the best place to find nursemaids, but blister

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:45.000
beetles have an amazing way of discovering them.

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:48.680
It starts simply enough with the female beetle.

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:57.040
She has dug a hole and is now laying her eggs in it.

00:23:57.040 --> 00:24:02.280
That done, she abandons them.

00:24:02.280 --> 00:24:06.960
A few centimeters below the surface of the sand, conditions are good for eggs, not too

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:13.440
cold, neither too hot, even in the heat of the day.

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:16.840
Six weeks later, they hatch.

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:20.360
But these sands are very barren and scorching hot.

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:29.520
Somehow the tiny larvae have got to find food and they won't find it here.

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:34.120
Their survival depends on teamwork.

00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:53.280
Together, as a closely coordinated group, they climb up a stem of withered grass.

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:56.600
When they get to the top, there's nowhere else to go.

00:24:56.600 --> 00:25:02.120
They look dangerously exposed to the sun and to other predators, but there they stay in

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:08.200
a tight squirming mass.

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:14.020
For those that can get there, the top of this stem has become a stage for a remarkable piece

00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:15.760
of deception.

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:21.520
What these larvae want is a lift, a ride, and they want it so badly that sometimes they'll

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:26.320
even try and get it from a human finger.

00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:29.840
What they're really searching for is not a human finger.

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:34.120
They're searching for another insect.

00:25:34.120 --> 00:25:42.040
Here it comes, a female digger bee, leaving a tunnel that she's just dug for her own young.

00:25:42.040 --> 00:25:50.400
She's off to gather pollen.

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:57.720
She packs it in two baskets on her back legs and takes it back to her burrow.

00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:06.960
To provide valuable food for her young when they eventually hatch.

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.880
And here comes a male.

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:16.200
He's on the lookout for a female.

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:21.560
To him, the cluster not only looks like a female, it smells like a female.

00:26:21.560 --> 00:26:26.760
For the beetle larvae are producing a perfume, a pheromone, that is exactly like that emitted

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:29.280
by a female bee.

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:34.800
He alights in order to mate and in seconds is covered by the larvae that swarm all over

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:38.120
him.

00:26:38.120 --> 00:26:44.240
At first he seems stunned by the shock of his sudden increase in weight.

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:46.800
But then he's off again.

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:50.320
Now his luck improves.

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:54.440
This really is a female.

00:26:54.440 --> 00:27:16.400
And while he mates, his passengers jump ship.

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:26.560
Now they're all on board a female bee.

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:35.800
She having mated goes back to her nest to lay, taking the larvae with her.

00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:42.160
At last the young beetle larvae have reached safety and food, the sort of pollen that the

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:47.480
female digger bee worked so hard to collect for her own young.

00:27:47.480 --> 00:27:56.480
So they hop off and tuck in.

00:27:56.480 --> 00:28:01.320
Not only do they consume the pollen, when that runs out they will eat the young bee

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:05.320
larvae too.

00:28:05.320 --> 00:28:11.380
Blister beetles are not alone in using couriers to take their offspring to food.

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:17.120
The young of this bot fly here in Brazil feed on the blood and tissues of living cows.

00:28:17.120 --> 00:28:20.480
But how is a female to get them there?

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:26.320
She is a big insect, so big that cows would notice if she landed on them and would probably

00:28:26.320 --> 00:28:27.820
flick her off.

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:30.720
She needs a lightweight courier.

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:37.680
A house fly, a fraction of her weight, that will do nicely.

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:42.200
She drops down to stalk it.

00:28:42.200 --> 00:28:48.200
She got it.

00:28:48.200 --> 00:28:51.800
She manipulates the house fly into the right position.

00:28:51.800 --> 00:29:02.160
And now one by one she glues her eggs onto the house fly's abdomen.

00:29:02.160 --> 00:29:10.440
Within a few seconds the house fly has been coated by about 30 cream coloured eggs.

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:15.520
The bot fly releases its hapless messenger.

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:30.640
The house fly seems well aware that it's carrying an extra load, but it can't get rid of it.

00:29:30.640 --> 00:29:40.080
So it goes back to its normal business, which includes visiting cows to drink their sweat.

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:46.020
A small fly, unlike the numbering bot fly, is no real irritation and is able to feed

00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:50.960
largely unhindered.

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:59.640
The fly mops up the sweat with its pad-shaped mouthparts, but as it feeds so the warmth

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:09.880
of the cow's body causes the bot fly's eggs to hatch.

00:30:09.880 --> 00:30:15.880
The larvae are armed with tiny hooks which help them to get a grip on a cow's skin and

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:23.400
bore into it.

00:30:23.400 --> 00:30:31.760
So in a few minutes a cow can acquire a dozen bot fly larvae feeding away beneath its skin.

00:30:31.760 --> 00:30:36.280
Licking won't get rid of them now.

00:30:36.280 --> 00:30:43.320
A couple of months later the full grown larvae emerge and drop to the ground.

00:30:43.320 --> 00:30:51.360
There they will burrow into the soil, pupate and turn into adults.

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:57.360
All kinds of creatures, great and small, are exploited by insect parents in this kind of

00:30:57.360 --> 00:30:58.360
way.

00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:03.400
This is Costa Rica and here lives a species of orchard spider.

00:31:03.400 --> 00:31:10.640
They construct horizontal orb webs as lovely as those made by any spider.

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:14.480
But one individual has a hanger on.

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:24.440
One anonymous looking grub is clinging to her abdomen.

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:31.280
She seems little affected by having a passenger and every day as usual she builds a new and

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:34.980
perfect web.

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:41.120
She is just as efficient a hunter as ever, but every catch she makes she shares in effect

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:58.920
with her passenger, for the grub is sucking her juices.

00:31:58.920 --> 00:32:07.480
Her passenger stays with her for some two weeks, slowly growing in size at her expense.

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:13.000
And still daily she constructs a new web.

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:18.640
Then one evening when as usual she starts to spin, something seems to have gone dramatically

00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:25.240
wrong.

00:32:25.240 --> 00:32:29.000
She seems incapable of making her normal beautiful orb.

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:34.240
What she produces has no shape, no radiating spokes, no sticky spawn.

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:37.360
It's just an untidy tangle.

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:39.560
The grub is responsible.

00:32:39.560 --> 00:32:49.540
It has injected her with a hormone that has spread to her brain and deranged her.

00:32:49.540 --> 00:32:52.380
She has only an hour or so to live.

00:32:52.380 --> 00:32:55.240
This is her last act.

00:32:55.240 --> 00:32:57.840
Small claspers inflate on the grub's back.

00:32:57.840 --> 00:33:02.800
With these it grasps the wreckage of the web so that it will not fall as the dying spider

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:08.480
loses her grip.

00:33:08.480 --> 00:33:17.000
It sucks the remaining fluid from the spider's body.

00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:24.440
Slowly the liquid is withdrawn.

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:33.120
Then the spider's legs are emptied until the corpse is no more than a husk.

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:37.800
The grub has no further use for it.

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:47.640
And now the grub clinging to the spider's last tangled web starts to spin for itself.

00:33:47.640 --> 00:34:01.440
It needs a shelter in which to reorganize its body, a cocoon.

00:34:01.440 --> 00:34:07.920
Inside the lacy walls its body is breaking down, for it has to be reassembled in a very

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:11.400
different form.

00:34:11.400 --> 00:34:30.880
At last the killer is about to reveal its true identity.

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:33.500
It's a wasp.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:40.000
Now it must fly off to find a mate so that another wasp egg may be attached to another

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:49.800
orchard spider.

00:34:49.800 --> 00:34:56.240
The opportunity to find creatures to parasitize in the undergrowth seem almost endless.

00:34:56.240 --> 00:35:02.240
And yet surprisingly there are some parasitic wasps that find their victims in water, in

00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:05.200
lakes and ponds like this one.

00:35:05.200 --> 00:35:08.560
They're extremely small, about a quarter of a millimeter long.

00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:11.360
In fact one of the smallest of all insects.

00:35:11.360 --> 00:35:14.600
And I've got some in this test tube.

00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:20.280
And to give you an idea of just how small they are, I'll drop this pin in alongside

00:35:20.280 --> 00:35:24.200
them to give a sense of scale.

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:39.240
But these tiny specks have eyes, legs, feelers, just like any other insect.

00:35:39.240 --> 00:35:44.720
They're known as fairy wasps and spend nearly all their lives underwater.

00:35:44.720 --> 00:35:51.520
They make a tiny water flea itself only the size of a grain of sand look like a giant.

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:57.240
And so minute they can lay their eggs inside the eggs of other insects and they choose

00:35:57.240 --> 00:36:03.520
those laid by water beetles.

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:08.600
Water beetles lay their eggs inside plant stems.

00:36:08.600 --> 00:36:15.340
A female fairy wasp having located one uses its microscopically thin over positive to

00:36:15.340 --> 00:36:28.880
inject up to a hundred or so eggs into just one of the beetles.

00:36:28.880 --> 00:36:34.760
And here they hatch.

00:36:34.760 --> 00:36:44.360
Young wasps feed and grow consuming the water beetles undeveloped young.

00:36:44.360 --> 00:36:56.480
Not only that, they mate here.

00:36:56.480 --> 00:37:11.400
Then at last they leave the shell of the beetles egg.

00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:17.520
The females must now lay and some will be able to do so in other ponds because in spite

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:23.280
of everything, they still have wings.

00:37:23.280 --> 00:37:27.960
Other bigger parasitic wasps have totally lost their wings.

00:37:27.960 --> 00:37:31.720
You can find them on many a British heath.

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:37.400
This one, Mithochia, looks rather like an ant and insects that live by hunting ants

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:40.280
easily mistake it for one.

00:37:40.280 --> 00:37:44.040
The tiger beetle is a very active ant hunter.

00:37:44.040 --> 00:37:55.320
It chases them and runs them down.

00:37:55.320 --> 00:38:00.600
And very successful it is.

00:38:00.600 --> 00:38:06.000
Earlier in its life, of course, as a larva, a tiger beetle can't run around.

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:11.480
Instead, the larva catches ants by waiting in ambush.

00:38:11.480 --> 00:38:16.120
It plugs the entrance to its burrow with its armored plate like head.

00:38:16.120 --> 00:38:31.280
If an ant touches that, it's as good as dead.

00:38:31.280 --> 00:38:37.960
It works every time.

00:38:37.960 --> 00:38:55.720
Mithochia, however, is a more awkward customer.

00:38:55.720 --> 00:39:05.200
The beetle larva is waiting with jaws agape.

00:39:05.200 --> 00:39:10.520
But Mithochia is more agile than the usual ant and it manages to slip out between the

00:39:10.520 --> 00:39:16.080
beetle larva's jaws.

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:26.000
It grabs the larva's soft body and pulls.

00:39:26.000 --> 00:39:29.200
And now it stings it.

00:39:29.200 --> 00:39:41.720
Mithochia climbs out of the tunnel, waiting for the poison to take effect.

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:47.560
The sting has only paralyzed the larva and the wasp drags the helpless creature farther

00:39:47.560 --> 00:39:51.200
down its burrow.

00:39:51.200 --> 00:40:01.720
Now she lays her egg onto it.

00:40:01.720 --> 00:40:06.240
To prevent anything interfering with her grub while it stays underground, feeding on the

00:40:06.240 --> 00:40:26.800
paralyzed beetle larva, she blocks up the entrance.

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:31.360
This is the longest and most laborious part of her motherly duties.

00:40:31.360 --> 00:40:37.520
But now, without any more work from her, her young will have all the food it needs to develop

00:40:37.520 --> 00:40:44.680
into an adult.

00:40:44.680 --> 00:40:49.440
Underground nests are certainly among the best protected of all insect nurseries.

00:40:49.440 --> 00:40:55.440
And indeed, they're very difficult for parasites to break into.

00:40:55.440 --> 00:41:01.800
Ants defend their colonies against intruders with great ferocity.

00:41:01.800 --> 00:41:08.440
And yet, here in this meadow in central Europe, there are ant nests where intruders live undetected.

00:41:08.440 --> 00:41:19.360
And there's one right here.

00:41:19.360 --> 00:41:25.720
This is the caterpillar of a blue butterfly.

00:41:25.720 --> 00:41:32.640
And it's lived in this nest undetected and protected by the ants and fed by them for

00:41:32.640 --> 00:41:34.640
the last two years.

00:41:34.640 --> 00:41:40.920
Indeed, it's been so thoroughly accepted by the ants that they will try and rescue it

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:51.440
in preference to the young of their own queen, as in fact they're doing right now.

00:41:51.440 --> 00:42:02.640
But how do these caterpillars get into the ant's nest in the first place?

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:07.960
Alcon blue butterflies begin their courtship in June and July.

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:12.960
This is surely one of the lovely sights of a European summer as they flutter and flirt

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:25.280
among the flowers of the meadow.

00:42:25.280 --> 00:42:33.680
Male and female meet and join.

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:47.400
Once they have mated, the female Alcon blue must find a gentian plant.

00:42:47.400 --> 00:43:02.920
Here she lays her eggs.

00:43:02.920 --> 00:43:09.880
The caterpillars, when they hatch, stay feeding on the gentian for a couple of weeks.

00:43:09.880 --> 00:43:14.740
But eventually they fall to the ground.

00:43:14.740 --> 00:43:21.520
There are ants everywhere in a meadow like this and they soon find it.

00:43:21.520 --> 00:43:26.400
It smells just like one of their own larvae and they start to haul it back to where one

00:43:26.400 --> 00:43:31.000
of their larvae should be, in their nest.

00:43:31.000 --> 00:43:37.440
Other foragers from the same nest have found another.

00:43:37.440 --> 00:43:47.960
During the next few weeks, as many as half a dozen may be taken back to the nest.

00:43:47.960 --> 00:43:56.160
Here they're hauled down to the nursery chambers and put with the ants other eggs and larvae.

00:43:56.160 --> 00:44:01.000
And because the caterpillars continue to produce a pheromone exactly like that produced by

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:05.960
the young ants themselves, they're treated as if they were young ants, even though they're

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:10.080
bigger and a different colour.

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:16.200
The caterpillars even mimic the sound the ants make when they beg for food, so the workers

00:44:16.200 --> 00:44:24.160
dutifully feed and clean them.

00:44:24.160 --> 00:44:29.560
You might think that this caterpillar has protected itself very well by deceiving these

00:44:29.560 --> 00:44:37.640
ants, but life in the undergrowth is full of surprises.

00:44:37.640 --> 00:44:44.280
An ignuman wasp, it too, like the blue butterfly, wants to get its young into an ant's nest.

00:44:44.280 --> 00:44:50.200
But not merely as lodgers, it has a more sinister intention.

00:44:50.200 --> 00:44:55.200
Somehow or other, in a meadow full of ant's nests, it can detect which one harbours a

00:44:55.200 --> 00:45:01.160
butterfly caterpillar, and this, it decides, is one of those.

00:45:01.160 --> 00:45:06.320
Once inside, the ants start to attack it, as you might expect, but then the ant's behaviour

00:45:06.320 --> 00:45:07.320
changes.

00:45:07.320 --> 00:45:08.920
There's pandemonium.

00:45:08.920 --> 00:45:21.760
The wasp has released a pheromone that makes the ants attack one another.

00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:28.960
With the defenders fighting among themselves, the wasp is able to go deeper into the nest.

00:45:28.960 --> 00:45:32.400
It's reached the nursery, and here lie the caterpillars.

00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:41.300
Now they are defenceless.

00:45:41.300 --> 00:45:47.960
The wasp sets about injecting each of them with an egg.

00:45:47.960 --> 00:46:02.240
A few ants do their best to prevent this, but there is no real opposition.

00:46:02.240 --> 00:46:11.740
When most of the ants continue to fight among themselves, the wasp finds a second caterpillar.

00:46:11.740 --> 00:46:24.680
Another egg is laid.

00:46:24.680 --> 00:46:29.680
The wasp leaves.

00:46:29.680 --> 00:46:34.280
With the wasp gone, the ant colony slowly returns to normal.

00:46:34.280 --> 00:46:39.320
The caterpillars are still there, alive and apparently well, and the ants continue to

00:46:39.320 --> 00:46:41.640
care for them.

00:46:41.640 --> 00:46:46.920
Once the caterpillars are fully grown, each starts to construct the chrysalis, which all

00:46:46.920 --> 00:46:54.900
butterflies need as a protection while they turn themselves into adults.

00:46:54.900 --> 00:47:04.120
Each chrysalis is cleaned and protected by the ants as if it were one of their own pupae.

00:47:04.120 --> 00:47:17.920
One begins to hatch.

00:47:17.920 --> 00:47:25.680
Out of it comes, yes, a blue butterfly.

00:47:25.680 --> 00:47:31.160
It leaves its foster home.

00:47:31.160 --> 00:47:35.960
Out in the open, its limp wings can expand.

00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:44.240
And now it's ready to flutter and flirt, just as its parents did.

00:47:44.240 --> 00:47:49.920
And the ants are still bewitched by the traces of pheromone clinging to the empty shell the

00:47:49.920 --> 00:47:53.480
butterfly leaves behind.

00:47:53.480 --> 00:48:04.840
But there are still others in the nest, as yet, unhatched.

00:48:04.840 --> 00:48:19.640
And out of this one comes not a butterfly, but a wasp.

00:48:19.640 --> 00:48:24.880
Hardwired into the microscopic brain of this ordinary-looking insect are a whole series

00:48:24.880 --> 00:48:31.280
of skills, sensitivities, and reactions that will enable it, in its turn, to give its own

00:48:31.280 --> 00:48:34.160
offspring a special start in life.

00:48:34.160 --> 00:48:38.720
It can detect what the ants themselves find undetectable.

00:48:38.720 --> 00:48:43.960
It can tell the difference between an ant's larva and the larva of the butterfly, the

00:48:43.960 --> 00:48:45.520
caterpillar.

00:48:45.520 --> 00:48:52.320
In a meadow of 100 ants' nests, it can even detect the one nest that has the caterpillar

00:48:52.320 --> 00:48:53.560
in it.

00:48:53.560 --> 00:48:56.840
How it does that, we have no idea.

00:48:56.840 --> 00:49:02.280
So it seems that among the animals of the undergrowth, there are many mutually beneficial

00:49:02.280 --> 00:49:27.160
partnerships, but exploitation and deception can work just as well.

00:49:27.160 --> 00:49:33.400
They defend themselves with stings and very, very powerful stings, which is why I have

00:49:33.400 --> 00:49:36.920
to wear a bee suit.

00:49:36.920 --> 00:49:43.720
Because if one bee attacks you, within seconds, there will be hundreds, indeed, probably thousands

00:49:43.720 --> 00:49:50.960
of them all around you launching a mass attack and stinging you.

00:49:50.960 --> 00:49:56.160
From giant bees to marauding ant armies and termites in their fortress homes, we enter

00:49:56.160 --> 00:50:01.280
the world of the social insects.

00:50:01.280 --> 00:50:06.680
But understanding what's going on in these super societies, let alone filming them,

00:50:06.680 --> 00:50:13.520
wasn't ever going to be easy.

00:50:13.520 --> 00:50:16.500
These are giant Asiatic honeybees.

00:50:16.500 --> 00:50:21.120
In their colonies of tens of thousands, they're said to be the most dangerous animals in the

00:50:21.120 --> 00:50:23.760
Malaysian jungle.

00:50:23.760 --> 00:50:29.240
Once more, they live in the tops of very tall trees.

00:50:29.240 --> 00:50:33.000
And I am about to pay them a visit.

00:50:33.000 --> 00:50:37.960
But first, with such a potentially dangerous subject, is a thorough safety briefing from

00:50:37.960 --> 00:50:40.760
our expert, Nico Koeniger.

00:50:40.760 --> 00:50:43.600
He has studied bees for over 15 years.

00:50:43.600 --> 00:50:50.320
No, really, believe me, the bees are coming and if you're not protected, it's horrible.

00:50:50.320 --> 00:50:55.680
You would all wear bee suits, but that's not a protection in itself, as cameraman Gavin

00:50:55.680 --> 00:50:57.360
Thurston explains.

00:50:57.360 --> 00:51:01.600
Nico was telling us when he was up in a tree on ropes, resting against a branch, and the

00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:07.760
little branch broke, so he swung straight into one of those nests and he got attacked.

00:51:07.760 --> 00:51:14.200
And by the time he got down, he'd been stung 200 times through his bee suit and clothing.

00:51:14.200 --> 00:51:16.760
But that's where James Aldred comes in.

00:51:16.760 --> 00:51:21.800
He's the rigging expert and in charge of getting me up and down the tree.

00:51:21.800 --> 00:51:25.840
In the event of an attack, the bee suit will give me enough protection, so long as his

00:51:25.840 --> 00:51:29.080
system of ropes and pulleys can get me down to safety fast.

00:51:29.080 --> 00:51:33.120
Are you sure you still want to go through with it?

00:51:33.120 --> 00:51:35.800
Well, somebody ought to ask.

00:51:35.800 --> 00:51:39.480
The best precaution is in properly understanding the bees.

00:51:39.480 --> 00:51:42.560
And that's where Nico comes into his own.

00:51:42.560 --> 00:51:46.840
He's the first to go up.

00:51:46.840 --> 00:51:51.440
He visits the bees every day for his research and they become accustomed to ropes and bee

00:51:51.440 --> 00:51:56.640
suits, so he's confident that they won't be scared of me.

00:51:56.640 --> 00:52:04.440
However, for Gavin to get into a position to film, we had to bring in a crane.

00:52:04.440 --> 00:52:08.880
That's something that the bees have not seen before, so it's important to bring it in

00:52:08.880 --> 00:52:15.320
slowly whilst Nico watches the bees for any signs of agitation.

00:52:15.320 --> 00:52:20.000
He knows from experience that when they're calm, there's a regular traffic to and from

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:21.000
the comb.

00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:25.720
But when nervous, this stops and then they start dropping off the bottom of the comb

00:52:25.720 --> 00:52:34.960
in the pelude to an attack.

00:52:34.960 --> 00:52:39.760
From his position right beneath the comb, Nico is perfectly placed to watch the bees

00:52:39.760 --> 00:52:44.280
and give the call to get everyone down before things get serious.

00:52:44.280 --> 00:52:46.320
I'm in position.

00:52:46.320 --> 00:52:48.240
Bees are OK.

00:52:48.240 --> 00:52:55.720
Now all is set for my encounter with the bees.

00:52:55.720 --> 00:53:00.720
Meanwhile in southern Africa, we had a rather different challenge.

00:53:00.720 --> 00:53:07.440
These termite mounds may look like insect skyscrapers, but they're more than that.

00:53:07.440 --> 00:53:13.160
They are sophisticated air conditioning systems that somehow cool the colony, which is buried

00:53:13.160 --> 00:53:16.440
deep beneath the ground.

00:53:16.440 --> 00:53:21.360
For life in the undergrowth, we wanted to show how they work.

00:53:21.360 --> 00:53:27.120
Luckily for us, one man is trying to find out.

00:53:27.120 --> 00:53:30.680
By filling one with plaster.

00:53:30.680 --> 00:53:32.720
Six tons of it.

00:53:32.720 --> 00:53:35.400
One minute.

00:53:35.400 --> 00:53:40.840
Engineer Rupert Saw hopes that by completely filling a discarded mound with plaster, he

00:53:40.840 --> 00:53:43.240
can reveal its inner structure.

00:53:43.240 --> 00:53:45.120
Four minutes.

00:53:45.120 --> 00:53:49.240
It all needs to be timed with military precision before the plaster sets.

00:53:49.240 --> 00:53:50.240
That's it.

00:53:50.240 --> 00:53:51.240
Just coming up to nine minutes.

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:56.520
Another couple of minutes and that will pretty much be set.

00:53:56.520 --> 00:54:02.160
A quick incision, the outer casing comes off and he can blast away the mounds sunbaked

00:54:02.160 --> 00:54:04.600
mud with an industrial hose.

00:54:04.600 --> 00:54:13.600
You can really start to see some structure coming through now.

00:54:13.600 --> 00:54:16.280
This extraordinary structure is what's left.

00:54:16.280 --> 00:54:22.320
A plaster mold of the air spaces inside the mound.

00:54:22.320 --> 00:54:26.920
When you can see what the inside of a termite mound looks like, you can start to understand

00:54:26.920 --> 00:54:29.360
how it works.

00:54:29.360 --> 00:54:34.720
Back in England, Mick Conair from our graphic design team meets Rupert to hear his theory

00:54:34.720 --> 00:54:38.680
on how the air moves around inside this structure.

00:54:38.680 --> 00:54:41.800
This could be a chimney that's not actually just open to the outside.

00:54:41.800 --> 00:54:46.680
If there's enough air blowing through the top of the structure.

00:54:46.680 --> 00:55:01.600
Now his challenge is to make a working mound, not out of mud, but in a computer.

00:55:01.600 --> 00:55:06.920
Mick pieces it together and for the first time ever, we can start to see the termite

00:55:06.920 --> 00:55:11.040
air conditioning system in action.

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:16.280
Hot stale air from the colony below rises up through a central chimney.

00:55:16.280 --> 00:55:21.960
The wind then penetrates the mound and pumps that hot air out through the labyrinth of

00:55:21.960 --> 00:55:29.680
tubes, keeping the termites fresh and cool.

00:55:29.680 --> 00:55:34.480
With this graphic, we can show what a remarkable construction this is, especially considering

00:55:34.480 --> 00:55:43.220
it's made by termites out of nothing but spit and mud.

00:55:43.220 --> 00:55:48.680
In the rainforest, it's time for me to meet the bees.

00:55:48.680 --> 00:55:52.480
In the old days, I used to do this the hard way.

00:55:52.480 --> 00:56:01.640
Luckily for me, James has come up with an ingenious and less energetic solution.

00:56:01.640 --> 00:56:08.400
Okay.

00:56:08.400 --> 00:56:13.600
The jungle elevator.

00:56:13.600 --> 00:56:19.240
Good morning, Professor.

00:56:19.240 --> 00:56:24.240
Good morning, Dave.

00:56:24.240 --> 00:56:27.720
Little bit more.

00:56:27.720 --> 00:56:34.720
With Nicko at hand and James below, I'm confident, but I have to say it's an eerie feeling dangling

00:56:34.720 --> 00:56:41.160
up here on a thin rope, only an arm's length from tens of thousands of giant bees.

00:56:41.160 --> 00:56:45.400
For the colony, though, a mass attack is actually very costly.

00:56:45.400 --> 00:56:48.560
If a bee stings you, it dies.

00:56:48.560 --> 00:56:53.920
So they have evolved a quite remarkable behavior to warn off predators first.

00:56:53.920 --> 00:57:00.000
And that's what I really wanted to show without distressing the bees, without getting stung.

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:01.000
Okay.

00:57:01.000 --> 00:57:05.480
We'll do the first one.

00:57:05.480 --> 00:57:06.480
Camera speed.

00:57:06.480 --> 00:57:12.280
I've got a reproduction of a hornet, which is one of the main enemies of bees.

00:57:12.280 --> 00:57:15.200
I'll see if I can get them to do it.

00:57:15.200 --> 00:57:16.200
Just watch.

00:57:16.200 --> 00:57:17.200
There.

00:57:17.200 --> 00:57:23.200
See, there's a moving wave which passes over the surface of the colony.

00:57:23.200 --> 00:57:29.800
And that not only produces an impressive pattern, but it also makes it very difficult for any

00:57:29.800 --> 00:57:39.920
aggressor to actually land on that moving carpet of wings.

00:57:39.920 --> 00:57:40.920
I thought that was okay.

00:57:40.920 --> 00:57:41.920
Beautiful.

00:57:41.920 --> 00:57:45.440
Okay, David, if you could keep an eye on your safety, please.

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:46.440
I will.

00:57:46.440 --> 00:57:47.440
Thank you.

00:57:47.440 --> 00:57:49.160
And that was it.

00:57:49.160 --> 00:57:53.160
Thanks to Nicko and the bees, we got the shot.

00:57:53.160 --> 00:57:55.440
My encounter with the bees was over.

00:57:55.440 --> 00:57:56.440
What a relief.

00:57:56.440 --> 00:57:59.440
It was a bit of a dazzle, really.

00:57:59.440 --> 00:58:00.440
Thank you.
